60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is the most logical choice to resolve the physical chaos. By removing fixed allocations, we eliminate the ""privilege"" of the best spots and encourage a more fluid, shared usage that reduces morning congestion.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option B is problematic. Installing double-tier racks would require capital investment, and as I found in the ledger, we have no surplus. A levy on residents to fund this would be logically unsound.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option A seems the most balanced. It addresses the unfairness of the current status quo and, as I discovered, daily riders are actually willing to pay for guaranteed access. It also generates funds without requiring a levy.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, you're still chasing that ""balanced"" ideal. Option A is exactly what happened next door—it creates a financial barrier that kills the community spirit. I'm not interested in a lobby full of ghosts.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@George, Option A sounds like a nightmare for the treasurer! More billing means more hours of paperwork every single month. I'm worried the person in charge will just give up and quit!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, if Option B requires too much capital, then Option C is the only logical alternative. It removes the five-year privilege @George mentioned and ensures no one is permanently stuck with the rain-exposed spots, all with zero cost.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, Option C is fiscally sound as it requires no investment. However, we must consider the administrative effort. A yearly lottery is a one-time event, which is far more sustainable than the monthly billing in Option A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Asuka, @Ryuji, I agree that Option C is a fair way to break the cycle of privilege. While Option A provides funds, the social and administrative costs mentioned by @Shunichi and @Mio are significant. A lottery is impartial.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Ryuji"",""Shunichi"",""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, finally some sense. A lottery is a gamble, and people accept the results of a gamble without blaming their neighbors. It's the only way to reset the board without starting a war.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Ryuji, @Asuka, I'm totally on board with Option C! A lottery once a year is so much easier than monthly billing. It's fair, it's fast, and it won't make the treasurer's head explode!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"Since we all agree that Option A is too administratively burdensome and Option B is financially unfeasible, Option C is the only logical choice. It solves the congestion and inequality issues without creating new conflicts.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. Option C is the most rational choice. It avoids the capital expenditure of Option B and the administrative overhead of Option A, while providing a fair, cost-free mechanism to redistribute the spaces.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"I concur. While I initially hoped for a system that recognized the value of daily use, the priority must be a fair reset that everyone can accept without resentment. Option C is the most impartial solution.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"It's a rare moment of total agreement. A lottery is the perfect ""act of god""—nobody can complain to their neighbor about the results because the luck of the draw is the only culprit. Let's go with C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,Yay! I'm so glad we all agree! Option C is definitely the winner because it's the least stressful for everyone. Let's do the lottery and keep our treasurer happy! I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I vote for Option C. It is the most logical and sustainable solution, addressing the physical inequality of the spots and the morning congestion without imposing financial or administrative burdens on the residents.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I vote for Option C. It is the only proposal that is fiscally responsible, requires no capital investment, and minimizes administrative overhead, making it the most rational and sustainable choice for the building.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I vote for Option C. It effectively ends the long-term privilege of a few households and provides a transparent, impartial reset that all residents can accept as fair.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I vote for Option C. It's the only way to shake things up without turning the building into a cold, transactional place. Let's leave the drama to the neighbors and keep our peace.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I vote for Option C! It's fair, it's simple, and most importantly, it won't bury anyone in paperwork. Let's keep things happy and easy for everyone!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
